WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.606
By default, Orchard Core keeps uploaded media in the App_Data folder.

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:09.660
The Amazon S3 Media module keeps it in an S3 bucket instead.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.880
In this video, you configure the connection, enable the features, and follow an

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.030
uploaded file to the browser.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.255
Containers and clean deployments can remove the files on the local disk of

00:00:26.255 --> 00:00:30.417
a web server, and several instances of a site need the same media.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.770
An S3 bucket keeps the files outside the web server.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.823
The module has three features.

00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:41.814
Amazon Media Storage keeps the media files in a bucket, and Amazon Media

00:00:41.814 --> 00:00:44.950
Image Cache keeps resized copies of images.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:51.220
The third, marked Obsolete, is kept for backwards compatibility and enables the image cache.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.994
The site serves every file.

00:00:53.994 --> 00:00:58.961
A request reaches Orchard Core, which reads the object from the bucket into

00:00:58.961 --> 00:01:02.702
a local cache folder when no copy exists, and sends it.

00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:04.910
Asset URLs point to your site.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:15.473
Amazon Media Storage reads the Orchard Core, Media, Amazon S3 section of the configuration.

00:01:15.473 --> 00:01:19.409
It needs a bucket name, and a region or a service URL.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:25.050
If they are invalid, it logs an error and the site keeps its local file storage.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.330
Credentials come from the first source that has them:

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:35.133
the Credentials section, profiles, environment variables, then the role of the host in AWS.

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.500
Profiles and environment variables are preferred to keys in a file.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.684
Base Path stores the media in a folder of the bucket.

00:01:43.684 --> 00:01:46.012
Create Bucket is false by default.

00:01:46.012 --> 00:01:51.018
When true, a tenant that starts creates a missing bucket, without access control

00:01:51.018 --> 00:01:53.230
lists, and blocks public access.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:02:00.213
An environment variable overrides the application's configuration file, and a double underscore separates

00:02:00.213 --> 00:02:02.404
each level of the setting's path.

00:02:02.404 --> 00:02:06.840
Set the bucket name and the access keys that way, outside source control.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.894
To try this without an AWS account, run an S3 emulator in a

00:02:11.894 --> 00:02:14.012
container, such as S3Mock.

00:02:14.012 --> 00:02:18.713
Set the Service URL to its address, leave the region out, turn on

00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:23.480
Force Path Style, and enter dummy keys, which an emulator ignores.

00:02:27.693 --> 00:02:32.007
In the admin, open Tools, Features, and search for Amazon Media.

00:02:32.007 --> 00:02:36.383
The features of the module are listed under the Hosting category.

00:02:36.963 --> 00:02:38.923
Enable Amazon Media Storage.

00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:42.554
The Media Cache feature it depends on is enabled with it.

00:02:42.554 --> 00:02:46.993
With the settings in place, the media store is the bucket from then on.

00:02:48.613 --> 00:02:53.015
Settings, Media, Amazon S3 Options shows what the feature read.

00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:59.649
Bucket Name, Base Path, and Create Bucket are read-only, because they come from the configuration.

00:02:59.649 --> 00:03:02.073
The credentials are never shown.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.596
Settings, Media, Options confirms the active store.

00:03:06.596 --> 00:03:13.443
Storage provider reads Amazon S3, and Hierarchical namespace and Atomic move show crosses:

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:18.150
a bucket is flat, and a move is a copy followed by a delete.

00:03:22.372 --> 00:03:24.104
Open Media, Library.

00:03:24.104 --> 00:03:29.832
The library reads the bucket, which is empty, so the root folder lists no files.

00:03:30.303 --> 00:03:35.070
Choose Upload files, and select the picture that the home page uses as its banner.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.537
The site writes it to the bucket as an object, and keeps a

00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:40.433
copy in a local cache folder.

00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:42.117
Open the file.

00:03:42.117 --> 00:03:47.580
Its address begins with the media path of your site, not the address of the bucket.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.447
A request for a file with no local copy reads it from the

00:03:51.447 --> 00:03:55.069
bucket, and Settings, Media, Cache purges the copies.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:05.206
When the settings are valid, the feature registers the S3 client, replaces IMediaFileStore

00:04:05.206 --> 00:04:10.092
with a store over an S3 file store, and serves files from a cache folder.

00:04:10.092 --> 00:04:12.689
Otherwise it registers none of this.

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:17.961
When Media TUS Uploads is enabled and the media settings are valid, a

00:04:17.961 --> 00:04:22.724
separate startup class replaces the temporary store of resumable uploads.

00:04:22.724 --> 00:04:28.029
Partial uploads are then staged in the media bucket, instead of on the local disk.

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:37.948
Amazon Media Image Cache has its own Image Cache section, inside the Amazon S3 section.

00:04:37.948 --> 00:04:42.313
It sets a bucket of its own, with creation and removal, and takes

00:04:42.313 --> 00:04:46.679
the region and the other AWS settings from the Amazon S3 section.

00:04:47.429 --> 00:04:52.216
It replaces IResizedImageCache with an implementation backed by the bucket.

00:04:52.216 --> 00:04:57.121
The resized images survive the replacement of the web server's disk, and that

00:04:57.121 --> 00:04:59.839
disk is spared reading and writing them.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.844
Enable Amazon Media Image Cache in Tools, Features.

00:05:04.844 --> 00:05:09.334
It depends on Amazon Media Storage, and each feature uses a bucket only

00:05:09.334 --> 00:05:11.550
when its own settings are valid.

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.406
Open the home page.

00:05:13.406 --> 00:05:18.987
Its banner is the uploaded picture, which the theme resizes with a media profile named banner.

00:05:18.987 --> 00:05:23.285
The first request resizes it and writes the result to the cache bucket,

00:05:23.285 --> 00:05:25.741
and later requests read it from there.

00:05:29.941 --> 00:05:35.924
Bucket Name and Base Path accept Liquid, and ShellSettings holds the name of the tenant.

00:05:35.924 --> 00:05:40.315
Use it for a bucket per tenant, or a folder in a shared bucket.

00:05:40.315 --> 00:05:45.471
The name is used as rendered, so it must already be a valid S3 bucket name.

00:05:46.221 --> 00:05:50.029
By default, removing a tenant leaves the bucket alone.

00:05:50.029 --> 00:05:55.424
Remove Bucket asks S3 to delete the bucket, without deleting its objects first,

00:05:55.424 --> 00:05:58.026
and reports an error if S3 refuses.

00:05:58.026 --> 00:06:01.581
The image cache has its own Remove Bucket setting.

00:06:03.581 --> 00:06:05.971
That is Amazon S3 Media Storage:

00:06:05.971 --> 00:06:11.445
media files in a bucket, resized images in another, and settings for each tenant.

00:06:11.445 --> 00:06:13.081
Thanks for watching.

